// Practice quiz: Reading: cookies vs localStorage, where tokens should live
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/fetch-and-browser-client/cookies-vs-localstorage-where-tokens-should-live

::o1 script access::Why is a session token in localStorage exposed to cross-site scripting? {
=Any script running on the origin can read it #Correct. Injected script runs as your origin.
~localStorage is sent with every request #Not quite. It is never sent automatically.
~localStorage is shared between all websites #Not quite. It is separated by origin.
~It expires when the tab closes #Not quite. That is sessionStorage, and it is unrelated.
}

::o1 remaining risk::A session is kept in an HttpOnly cookie. What can injected script still do? {
=Send requests from the page that carry the cookie #Correct. The browser attaches it, so the script acts as the user.
~Read the cookie value with document.cookie #Not quite. HttpOnly hides it.
~Nothing at all #Not quite. It still runs in your page.
~Change the HttpOnly flag from script #Not quite. Script cannot see or change that cookie.
}

::o2 secure::What does the Secure attribute do? {
=The cookie is sent only on HTTPS requests #Correct. It keeps the cookie off plain HTTP.
~The cookie value is encrypted by the browser #Not quite. Secure controls the transport; it does not encrypt the value.
~The cookie cannot be read by script #Not quite. That is HttpOnly.
~The cookie is limited to the same site #Not quite. That is SameSite.
}

::o2 samesite strict::With SameSite\=Strict, when is the cookie sent? {
=Only on requests that start from the same site #Correct. Cross-site requests do not carry it, even following a link.
~On all requests #Not quite. That would be SameSite\=None.
~Only on GET requests #Not quite. Lax has a top-level navigation allowance, but Strict is not about methods.
~Never #Not quite. It is sent on same-site requests.
}

::o2 samesite none::What do browsers require for a cookie with SameSite\=None? {
=It must also be marked Secure #Correct. Browsers reject SameSite\=None cookies without Secure.
~It must also be HttpOnly #Not quite. That is recommended for sessions, but not required by this rule.
~It must have no expiry #Not quite. Expiry is unrelated.
~It must be set from script #Not quite. Servers can set it with a header.
}
